  "context": "## Before Digital Nomads, There Were Distributed Tiger Teams\nThe distributed-model origins locate the practice before remote work became a lifestyle category. State which records, constraints and decision rights had to remain usable as the team moved. Location and connectivity are means; the maintained ability to understand and commit the work is the operating test.\n\nThe early JubAp.Net lineage was shaped by distributed R&D, mobility infrastructure and cross-border operational continuity long before remote work became a lifestyle category. Distribution was an engineering requirement, not a perk.\n\nWhen people speak today about digital nomadism, they usually imagine laptops on café tables, co-working spaces, and remote work as a lifestyle choice.\n\nThe early JubAp.Net lineage was shaped by a much more demanding form of distributed work: high-technology information management, mobile infrastructure, remote expert coordination and operational continuity across borders, long before remote work became normalised.\n\nThe founder, Iván Abril Palma, had previously worked as Information Manager in Nokia’s distributed R&D environment in Barcelona. This was not simply about carrying a laptop. It involved the information, infrastructure and coordination logic required to support advanced research and development teams working across mobility, communications and distributed technical environments at the edge of what was possible at the time.\nTags: Business and management 24.14%; Geopolitics and borders 17.24%; Mobility and infrastructure 24.14%; Transformation and operational continuity 34.48%\nCard: https://dakleyer.github.io/perspective-beyond/sources/before-digital-nomads-there-were-distributed-tiger-teams-n1261/\nSource document: https://jubap.net/before-digital-nomads-there-were-distributed-tiger-teams/\n\n",
